[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Split a node type when one field changes its semantics

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-SPLIT-TYPE-ON-SEMANTIC-FIELD
TITLE: Split a node type when one field changes its semantics
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A grammar design rule the operator stated: when a single field's value
changes the semantic rules that apply to a node, consider splitting the
type instead, so that each type carries one set of rules and no reader has
to inspect a field to know which rules are in force.

The split doubles as the visual cue. The board colours by node type, so
semantics show without reading fields -- a GAP is red because it is a GAP,
not because a kind field says so. "Gap, red, bad, do not pass go."

Applies first to the annotation kinds (WORK-ANNOTATION-NODES): it is the
reason the operator leans node-type over kind-field there. It also bears on
DEPTH/STATUS-style ladders, where one field's value already selects which
rules apply; whether any of those should split is not decided here.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Operator, 2026-09-01, verbatim: "actually i think i also said that session
that if a single field changes the semantics of a node, we should consider
the split simply to divide the semantic rules. and it doubles as the visual
cue I wanted cuz it's based on the semantics (gap, red, bad, do not pass
go)."
<<<
